const LESSONS = [
  {slug:"what-is-aviation",wp:"WELCM",title:"What Is Aviation?",path:"foundations",diff:1,mins:8,
   intro:"Aviation is everything humans do with flying machines — designing them, flying them, and running the global system that keeps 100,000 flights a day moving safely. This lesson maps the territory you're about to explore.",
   sections:[
     {h:"The three worlds of aviation",p:"Aviation splits into three overlapping worlds. <b>Commercial aviation</b> is the airline system — moving people and cargo for money. <b>General aviation</b> is everything personal and small: training flights, private aircraft, crop dusting, medical flights. <b>Military aviation</b> covers defense, from fighters to transport. Nearly every aircraft in our database belongs to one of these worlds, and each world shaped different machines."},
     {h:"A system, not just machines",p:"An aircraft alone is useless. Aviation works because of an invisible system: airports with precisely numbered runways, air traffic controllers sequencing arrivals seconds apart, weather forecasting, navigation networks, and rules agreed by nearly every country on Earth through ICAO. When you fly from Delhi to Boston, dozens of these systems hand you off seamlessly."},
     {h:"Why safety defines everything",p:"Commercial aviation is the safest form of transport ever engineered — and that isn't luck. Every crash is investigated, every lesson becomes a rule, and every rule stays. This 'never forget' culture is why a system moving billions of people has years with zero fatal jet accidents."}],
   check:[{q:"Which of these belongs to general aviation?",opts:["An Emirates A380 flight","A student's first solo in a Cessna 172","An F-22 patrol"],a:1,ex:"Flight training and personal flying are general aviation — the world where nearly every pilot starts."},
     {q:"True or false: aviation safety rules mostly come from lessons learned in past accidents.",opts:["True","False"],a:0,ex:"True. Aviation's safety record comes from investigating every failure and permanently encoding the lesson."}],
   summary:["Aviation = commercial + general + military worlds","Aircraft depend on a global invisible system (airports, ATC, navigation, rules)","Safety culture — investigate everything, forget nothing — defines the industry"],
   related:["four-forces"]},

  {slug:"four-forces",wp:"FORCE",title:"The Four Forces of Flight",path:"foundations",diff:1,mins:12,interactive:"forces",
   intro:"Every aircraft, from a paper plane to an A380, is a negotiation between exactly four forces: lift, weight, thrust, and drag. Understand these four and you understand why every aircraft looks the way it does.",
   sections:[
     {h:"The tug of war",p:"<b>Weight</b> pulls the aircraft down — gravity acting on every kilogram. <b>Lift</b>, made by the wings, pushes up. <b>Thrust</b> from engines pulls forward. <b>Drag</b> — air resistance — pulls back. In steady, level flight the pairs balance perfectly: lift equals weight, thrust equals drag. Change any one, and the aircraft climbs, descends, speeds up, or slows down."},
     {h:"Try it below",p:"The diagram under this section is live. Drag the throttle slider and watch the balance change: more thrust than drag and the aircraft accelerates; the extra speed makes more lift; more lift than weight and it climbs. This is genuinely how flying works — pilots manage energy, and the forces do the rest."},
     {h:"Why designs differ",p:"Every aircraft is a different answer to the four-force problem. The An-225 needed six engines because 640 tonnes of weight demands enormous lift, which demands a huge wing, which makes huge drag, which demands huge thrust. The SR-71 minimized drag above all, so it could outrun anything. A Cessna 172 keeps all four forces small and gentle — perfect for learning."}],
   check:[{q:"In steady level flight, lift is equal to…",opts:["Thrust","Drag","Weight"],a:2,ex:"Lift balances weight; thrust balances drag. Two separate tugs of war."},
     {q:"If thrust becomes greater than drag, the aircraft will…",opts:["Slow down","Accelerate","Descend"],a:1,ex:"Unbalanced forward force means acceleration — Newton's second law at work."},
     {q:"Which aircraft's design was dominated by minimizing drag?",opts:["Antonov An-225","Lockheed SR-71","Cessna 172"],a:1,ex:"The Blackbird's entire shape is a war on drag — that's how it sustained Mach 3+."}],
   summary:["Four forces: lift ↑, weight ↓, thrust →, drag ←","Level flight = lift equals weight, thrust equals drag","Every aircraft design is a different balance of the four"],
   related:["how-wings-lift","drag"]},

  {slug:"how-wings-lift",wp:"BERNU",title:"How Wings Make Lift",path:"foundations",diff:2,mins:14,interactive:"lift",
   intro:"A 575-tonne A380 is held up by nothing but air. Here's how a wing actually does it — and what happens when you ask it for too much.",
   sections:[
     {h:"Turning air downward",p:"A wing makes lift by doing one thing: <b>deflecting air downward</b>. Its curved shape and slight upward tilt (the <b>angle of attack</b>) force the air flowing over and under it to leave moving down. Newton's third law does the rest — push air down, and the air pushes the wing up. Pressure tells the same story from another angle: air moves faster over the curved top surface, and faster-moving air has lower pressure, so the wing is squeezed upward."},
     {h:"Angle of attack — the throttle of lift",p:"Tilt the wing up a little more and it deflects more air: more lift. This is why aircraft point nose-up on takeoff. Use the interactive diagram below — drag the slider to increase the angle of attack and watch lift grow… up to a point."},
     {h:"The stall",p:"Past roughly 15°, the smooth airflow can no longer follow the wing's upper surface. It tears away into turbulence, lift collapses, and the wing <b>stalls</b>. A stall isn't the engine failing — it's the wing failing. Every pilot trains extensively to recognize it and recover: lower the nose, reduce the angle, and the air reattaches. Try it in the diagram — push the slider past the red line."}],
   check:[{q:"A wing generates lift primarily by…",opts:["Being lighter than air","Deflecting air downward","Pushing exhaust down"],a:1,ex:"Deflect air down, get pushed up — Newton's third law. The pressure difference is the same effect described differently."},
     {q:"A stall happens when…",opts:["The engine stops","The angle of attack gets too high and airflow separates","The aircraft flies too high"],a:1,ex:"Stall = airflow separation from too much angle of attack. It can happen at any speed and any altitude."},
     {q:"The first response to a stall is to…",opts:["Pull up harder","Lower the nose to reduce angle of attack","Add flaps"],a:1,ex:"Reduce the angle, let the airflow reattach. Pulling up deepens the stall."}],
   summary:["Lift = air deflected downward (Newton) = pressure difference (Bernoulli) — same physics, two views","More angle of attack → more lift, until ~15°","Beyond that: airflow separates → stall → recover by lowering the nose"],
   related:["four-forces","drag"]},

  {slug:"drag",wp:"SLIPS",title:"Drag: The Tax on Flight",path:"foundations",diff:2,mins:10,
   intro:"Drag is the price the air charges for moving through it. Every design decision in aviation — every curve, winglet, and polished rivet — is about paying less of this tax.",
   sections:[
     {h:"Two kinds of tax",p:"<b>Parasite drag</b> is the cost of pushing your shape through air — it grows with the <i>square</i> of speed, which is why going twice as fast costs four times the drag. <b>Induced drag</b> is the cost of making lift itself: deflecting air downward inevitably stirs it sideways too, especially at the wingtips, where high-pressure air below spills around to the top and forms trailing vortices."},
     {h:"Why wingtips curl up",p:"Those upturned tips on modern jets — winglets and sharklets — are fences against the wingtip spill. By blocking part of the vortex, they cut induced drag by several percent. A few percent sounds small until you multiply it by a 15-hour flight and 140 tonnes of fuel: winglets pay for themselves fast."},
     {h:"The designer's dilemma",p:"Here's the elegant tension: induced drag <i>shrinks</i> as you fly faster (the wing needs less angle of attack), while parasite drag <i>grows</i>. Add them and you get a U-shaped curve with a sweet spot at the bottom — the speed of minimum total drag. Long-range aircraft are engineered so their most efficient speed is exactly where the airline wants to cruise."}],
   check:[{q:"Parasite drag grows with the ___ of speed.",opts:["square","logarithm","cube root"],a:0,ex:"Double the speed, quadruple the parasite drag — the tyranny of v²."},
     {q:"Winglets reduce which kind of drag?",opts:["Parasite drag","Induced drag"],a:1,ex:"They block wingtip vortex spill — a cost of making lift, i.e., induced drag."}],
   summary:["Parasite drag: cost of shape, grows with v²","Induced drag: cost of lift, strongest when slow","Total drag is U-shaped → every aircraft has a most-efficient speed"],
   related:["how-wings-lift","jet-engines"]},

  {slug:"jet-engines",wp:"SPOOL",title:"How Jet Engines Work",path:"foundations",diff:2,mins:12,
   intro:"Suck, squeeze, bang, blow — four words that describe every jet engine ever built. Here's the machinery behind the world's most reliable source of thrust.",
   sections:[
     {h:"The four steps",p:"A jet engine is a continuous cycle. <b>Suck:</b> the fan and compressor pull in enormous amounts of air. <b>Squeeze:</b> compressor stages squash it to 40–50× atmospheric pressure, heating it in the process. <b>Bang:</b> fuel sprays in and burns continuously (not in explosions — a steady 2,000°C flame). <b>Blow:</b> the hot gas blasts backward through turbines — which, crucially, are what spin the fan and compressor at the front. The engine powers itself."},
     {h:"The turbofan trick",p:"Modern airliners use <b>turbofans</b>, where the giant front fan pushes most air <i>around</i> the hot core rather than through it. This 'bypass' air is the secret to efficiency and quiet: accelerating a lot of air a little beats accelerating a little air a lot. The GEnx on a 747-8 sends about nine times more air around the core than through it — a bypass ratio of 9:1."},
     {h:"Why they almost never fail",p:"A turbofan's blades spin at up to 10,000+ RPM with tips going supersonic, in gas hotter than the melting point of the metal (tiny air channels inside each turbine blade keep them alive). Yet modern engines fail so rarely — roughly once per hundreds of thousands of flight hours — that twin-engine jets are trusted to cross oceans hours from any runway. That trust, called ETOPS, reshaped the entire industry around efficient twins like the 787 and A350."}],
   check:[{q:"What spins the big fan at the front of a turbofan?",opts:["An electric motor","Turbines driven by exhaust gas","Ram air pressure"],a:1,ex:"Exhaust spins the turbines; the turbines drive the shaft; the shaft spins the fan and compressor. Self-powering."},
     {q:"High 'bypass ratio' means…",opts:["Most air goes around the core, not through it","The engine skips the compressor","Fuel bypasses the combustor"],a:0,ex:"Bypass air = efficiency and quiet. Modern ratios run 9:1 and beyond."},
     {q:"ETOPS rules allowed…",opts:["Supersonic flight over land","Twin-engine jets to fly long ocean routes","Pilotless aircraft"],a:1,ex:"Proven engine reliability let twins cross oceans — which is why the 787 and A350 replaced four-engine giants."}],
   summary:["Cycle: suck → squeeze → bang → blow, self-powered by its own exhaust","Turbofan bypass air = efficiency + quiet","Reliability (ETOPS) is why twins now rule the long-haul skies"],
   related:["drag","four-forces"]},

  {slug:"runway-numbers",wp:"ROGER",title:"Runway Numbers Decoded",path:"foundations",diff:1,mins:8,interactive:"runway",
   intro:"Runway 27L isn't the 27th runway — it's a compass heading in disguise. After this lesson, every runway number in the world will make sense to you.",
   sections:[
     {h:"It's a compass bearing",p:"Take the runway's magnetic heading, round to the nearest 10°, drop the zero. A runway pointing 274° magnetic becomes <b>Runway 27</b>. Pointing due east (090°)? <b>Runway 09</b>. And because every strip of pavement works in both directions, each runway has two numbers exactly 18 apart (180°): the other end of Runway 27 is Runway 09."},
     {h:"L, R, and C",p:"Big airports have parallel runways with the same heading, so letters distinguish them: <b>L</b>eft, <b>R</b>ight, <b>C</b>enter as seen by a landing pilot. Atlanta's five parallels include 27L and 27R. When an airport has more parallels than three, some are renumbered one digit off — Los Angeles uses 24 and 25 for runways that are nearly parallel."},
     {h:"Why they land into the wind",p:"Aircraft take off and land into the wind — headwind gives the wing extra airspeed for free, shortening the ground run. That's why airports switch runway directions when the wind shifts: Heathrow flips between 'westerly ops' (27L/27R) and 'easterly ops' (09L/09R) depending on the day's wind. Try the converter below: enter any heading and see its runway number."}],
   check:[{q:"A runway pointing 094° magnetic is numbered…",opts:["94","09","19"],a:1,ex:"Round to 090, drop the zero: Runway 09."},
     {q:"The opposite end of Runway 31 is…",opts:["Runway 13","Runway 03","Runway 62"],a:0,ex:"Add or subtract 18 (i.e., 180°): 31 − 18 = 13."},
     {q:"Aircraft prefer to land…",opts:["With the wind behind them","Into the wind","In zero wind only"],a:1,ex:"Headwind = free airspeed = shorter, safer landing."}],
   summary:["Runway number = magnetic heading ÷ 10, rounded","Opposite ends differ by 18 (180°)","L/C/R distinguish parallels · aircraft operate into the wind"],
   related:["what-is-aviation"]}
];

