{slug:"boeing-777-9",name:"Boeing 777-9",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"commercial",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"prototype",first:2020,crew:2,pax:426,len:76.7,span:71.8,hgt:19.7,cruise:905,vmax:950,range:13500,ceil:13140,mtow:351500,engines:"2 × GE9X turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The longest airliner ever built, with wings so wide the tips fold upward on the ground to fit standard gates — a first for a commercial jet. Currently in flight testing ahead of service entry.",trivia:["Its folding wingtips add 7 m of span in flight, then tuck in to fit airport gates.","The GE9X is the largest jet engine ever built, yet quieter than its predecessors."],related:["boeing-777-300er","airbus-a350-1000"]},
{slug:"boeing-767-300er",name:"Boeing 767-300ER",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"commercial",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:1986,crew:2,pax:269,len:54.9,span:47.6,hgt:15.8,cruise:851,vmax:913,range:11070,ceil:13140,mtow:186880,engines:"2 × PW4000 / CF6-80 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The quiet pioneer of transatlantic twinjet flying — the 767 did more than any aircraft to establish the ETOPS rules that reshaped aviation. Passenger versions are fading, but freighters and tankers will fly for decades.",trivia:["The first twinjet certified for 180-minute ETOPS overwater flying (1989).","The US Air Force's new KC-46 tanker is a 767 underneath."],related:["boeing-757-200","airbus-a330-300"]},
{slug:"boeing-757-200",name:"Boeing 757-200",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"commercial",role:"Narrow-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:1982,crew:2,pax:200,len:47.3,span:38.1,hgt:13.6,cruise:850,vmax:913,range:7250,ceil:12800,mtow:115680,engines:"2 × RB211 / PW2000 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The 'flying pencil' — a long, overpowered narrow-body with legendary climb performance that airlines still miss. Nothing built since matches its mix of range and runway performance, which is why 40-year-old 757s still cross the Atlantic daily.",trivia:["So overpowered it's a favorite for hot-and-high airports like Bogotá and Denver.","Boeing never built a true successor — the 'middle of the market' gap it left is still debated."],related:["boeing-767-300er","airbus-a321neo"]},
{slug:"boeing-727-200",name:"Boeing 727-200",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"historic",role:"Narrow-body trijet",status:"retired",first:1963,crew:3,pax:149,len:46.7,span:32.9,hgt:10.4,cruise:867,vmax:961,range:4020,ceil:12800,mtow:95028,engines:"3 × JT8D turbofan (tail-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:3,m:"tail",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The trijet that brought jets to smaller cities — three tail-mounted engines, a T-tail, and built-in airstairs meant it needed no ground equipment and shorter runways. Over 1,800 built made it the best-selling jetliner of its era.",trivia:["Its rear airstairs became infamous via the D.B. Cooper hijacking — leading to the 'Cooper vane' lock fitted fleet-wide.","Could operate from gravel strips with a special kit."],related:["boeing-737-800","tupolev-tu-154"]},
{slug:"boeing-707-320",name:"Boeing 707-320",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"historic",role:"Narrow-body airliner",status:"retired",first:1957,crew:3,pax:189,len:46.6,span:44.4,hgt:12.9,cruise:886,vmax:1010,range:9260,ceil:12800,mtow:151315,engines:"4 × JT3D turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:4,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The aircraft that made the Jet Age mainstream. The 707 shrank the Atlantic to seven hours, built Boeing's commercial empire, and set the template — swept wing, podded engines — that every airliner still follows.",trivia:["Test pilot Tex Johnston barrel-rolled the prototype over Lake Washington in 1955 — twice.","Air Force One was a 707 variant for nearly 30 years."],related:["douglas-dc-3","boeing-747-8"]},
{slug:"boeing-747-400",name:"Boeing 747-400",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"commercial",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:1988,crew:2,pax:416,len:70.7,span:64.4,hgt:19.4,cruise:913,vmax:988,range:13450,ceil:13100,mtow:396890,engines:"4 × CF6 / PW4000 / RB211 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:4,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low",d:"hump"},story:"The definitive Jumbo — the best-selling 747 variant and the queen of 1990s long-haul. It cut the flight engineer with a two-crew glass cockpit and added winglets; nearly 700 were built and freighter versions soldier on.",trivia:["The -400 could carry enough fuel to fill about 4,800 car tanks.","Its upper deck was stretched, seating up to 52 more passengers than the classic hump."],related:["boeing-747-8","airbus-a380"]},
{slug:"boeing-737-max-8",name:"Boeing 737 MAX 8",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"commercial",role:"Narrow-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:2016,crew:2,pax:178,len:39.5,span:35.9,hgt:12.3,cruise:839,vmax:876,range:6570,ceil:12500,mtow:82191,engines:"2 × CFM LEAP-1B turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The fourth generation of the 737, with LEAP engines and distinctive split-tip winglets. Its two 2018–19 crashes and 20-month worldwide grounding became aviation's biggest modern reckoning on certification and software — and reshaped how regulators work.",trivia:["The MCAS software at the center of the grounding was revised and the fleet re-certified in late 2020.","Its split scimitar winglets save fuel in both directions — up and down."],related:["boeing-737-800","airbus-a320neo"]},
{slug:"airbus-a321neo",name:"Airbus A321neo",maker:"Airbus",country:"European Union",cat:"commercial",role:"Narrow-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:2016,crew:2,pax:200,len:44.5,span:35.8,hgt:11.8,cruise:833,vmax:871,range:7400,ceil:12000,mtow:97000,engines:"2 × LEAP-1A / PW1100G turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The aircraft eating the long-haul market from below — the A321neo and its LR/XLR versions fly routes like New York–Rome with single-aisle economics, killing the case for many widebodies. The best-selling large narrow-body in the world.",trivia:["The A321XLR version can fly up to 8,700 km — true transatlantic range from a single-aisle jet.","Airlines have ordered more A321neos than any other Airbus model."],related:["airbus-a320neo","boeing-757-200"]},
{slug:"airbus-a330-300",name:"Airbus A330-300",maker:"Airbus",country:"European Union",cat:"commercial",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:1992,crew:2,pax:277,len:63.7,span:60.3,hgt:16.8,cruise:871,vmax:913,range:11750,ceil:12500,mtow:242000,engines:"2 × Trent 700 / CF6 / PW4000 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"Airbus's long-serving widebody workhorse — over 1,500 A330s delivered, hauling everything from Asian trunk routes to transatlantic leisure traffic. The updated A330neo keeps the family in production three decades on.",trivia:["Shares its fuselage cross-section with the A340 — they were designed together as twin and quad siblings.","The A330 MRTT tanker version refuels fighters for a dozen air forces."],related:["airbus-a340-600","boeing-767-300er"]},
{slug:"airbus-a340-600",name:"Airbus A340-600",maker:"Airbus",country:"European Union",cat:"commercial",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:2001,crew:2,pax:326,len:75.4,span:63.4,hgt:17.9,cruise:881,vmax:913,range:14450,ceil:12500,mtow:380000,engines:"4 × Trent 500 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:4,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"For a few years the longest airliner in the world — a four-engine stretch built for ultra-long routes before big twins made quads uneconomic. Beloved by passengers, punished by fuel prices; only a handful still fly.",trivia:["So long it needed a tail-mounted camera just so pilots could taxi without clipping corners.","Its four relatively small engines earned the nickname 'four hairdryers'."],related:["airbus-a330-300","airbus-a350-1000"]},
{slug:"airbus-a350-1000",name:"Airbus A350-1000",maker:"Airbus",country:"European Union",cat:"commercial",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:2016,crew:2,pax:369,len:73.8,span:64.7,hgt:17.1,cruise:903,vmax:945,range:16100,ceil:13100,mtow:322000,engines:"2 × Trent XWB-97 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The stretched A350 and the spearhead of Qantas's coming 'Project Sunrise' flights — nonstop Sydney–London, over 20 hours in the air, the longest scheduled flights ever attempted.",trivia:["Project Sunrise A350-1000s will carry extra fuel tanks and a 'wellbeing zone' for 20+ hour flights.","Its Trent XWB-97 is the most powerful engine Rolls-Royce has ever built."],related:["airbus-a350-900","boeing-777-9"]},
{slug:"airbus-a300-600",name:"Airbus A300-600",maker:"Airbus",country:"European Union",cat:"historic",role:"Wide-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:1983,crew:2,pax:266,len:54.1,span:44.8,hgt:16.5,cruise:833,vmax:891,range:7500,ceil:12200,mtow:171700,engines:"2 × CF6 / PW4000 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The aircraft that started Airbus. The original A300 of 1972 was the world's first twin-engine widebody — a bet nobody believed in until it quietly became the template for every modern airliner. FedEx and UPS still fly A300 freighters daily.",trivia:["Early A300s sold so badly Airbus lent them to airlines for free — then Eastern Air Lines' fuel savings made the phone ring.","The A300-600ST 'Beluga' version carries Airbus wings between factories."],related:["airbus-a330-300","boeing-767-300er"]},
{slug:"airbus-a220-300",name:"Airbus A220-300",maker:"Airbus (Bombardier)",country:"EU / Canada",cat:"commercial",role:"Narrow-body airliner",status:"in_service",first:2015,crew:2,pax:141,len:38.7,span:35.1,hgt:11.5,cruise:829,vmax:871,range:6700,ceil:12500,mtow:70900,engines:"2 × PW1500G geared turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"Born as Bombardier's CSeries in Canada, acquired by Airbus after a trade war nearly killed it — and now quietly one of the best small airliners ever made: clean-sheet design, geared turbofans, big windows, and the quietest cabin in its class.",trivia:["A US tariff ruling of nearly 300% (later overturned) drove Bombardier to hand Airbus a majority stake for one dollar.","Passengers consistently rate its 3-2 seating and huge windows above bigger jets."],related:["embraer-e195-e2","airbus-a320neo"]},
{slug:"embraer-e195-e2",name:"Embraer E195-E2",maker:"Embraer",country:"Brazil",cat:"commercial",role:"Regional airliner",status:"in_service",first:2017,crew:2,pax:132,len:41.5,span:35.1,hgt:10.9,cruise:829,vmax:871,range:4815,ceil:12500,mtow:61500,engines:"2 × PW1900G geared turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"Brazil's masterpiece — the largest and most efficient member of the E-Jet family that made Embraer the world's third-largest airliner maker. Its 'profit hunter' marketing paint jobs (shark, eagle, tiger noses) are the boldest in the business.",trivia:["Embraer paints demo aircraft with full-nose animal liveries — the shark is famous at airshows.","Staggered 2-2 seating means no middle seats anywhere on board."],related:["airbus-a220-300","bombardier-crj900"]},
{slug:"bombardier-crj900",name:"Bombardier CRJ900",maker:"Bombardier",country:"Canada",cat:"commercial",role:"Regional jet",status:"in_service",first:2001,crew:2,pax:90,len:36.2,span:24.9,hgt:7.5,cruise:829,vmax:871,range:2876,ceil:12500,mtow:38330,engines:"2 × CF34-8C5 turbofan (tail-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"tail",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The backbone of North American regional flying — stretched from a business jet into an airliner family that connected thousands of small cities to the hub system. If you've flown to a small US airport, odds are it was on a CRJ.",trivia:["The CRJ family descends directly from the Challenger business jet.","Over 1,900 CRJs of all sizes were delivered before production ended in 2020."],related:["embraer-e195-e2","dash-8-q400"]},
{slug:"atr-72-600",name:"ATR 72-600",maker:"ATR",country:"France / Italy",cat:"commercial",role:"Regional turboprop",status:"in_service",first:1988,crew:2,pax:72,len:27.2,span:27.1,hgt:7.7,cruise:510,vmax:565,range:1528,ceil:7620,mtow:23000,engines:"2 × PW127 turboprop",p:{k:"prop",e:2,m:"wing",t:"t",w:"high"},story:"The world's best-selling regional aircraft — because on short hops, propellers simply beat jets on economics. ATRs connect islands, mountains, and small towns on every continent, burning ~40% less fuel than a regional jet on the same route.",trivia:["Sold in over 100 countries — the default aircraft of island-hopping airlines worldwide.","Its high wing keeps propellers clear of debris on rough regional strips."],related:["dash-8-q400","douglas-dc-3"]},
{slug:"dash-8-q400",name:"De Havilland Canada Dash 8 Q400",maker:"De Havilland Canada",country:"Canada",cat:"commercial",role:"Regional turboprop",status:"in_service",first:1998,crew:2,pax:82,len:32.8,span:28.4,hgt:8.4,cruise:667,vmax:685,range:2040,ceil:8230,mtow:30481,engines:"2 × PW150A turboprop",p:{k:"prop",e:2,m:"wing",t:"t",w:"high"},story:"The turboprop that thinks it's a jet — cruising 150 km/h faster than an ATR, with active noise cancellation (the 'Q' means quiet). Built for longer, busier regional routes where speed still matters.",trivia:["The 'Q' in Q400 stands for 'quiet' — tuned vibration absorbers hush the cabin.","Its six-bladed props swing slower than you'd guess: about 1,020 RPM in cruise."],related:["atr-72-600","bombardier-crj900"]},
{slug:"md-80",name:"McDonnell Douglas MD-80",maker:"McDonnell Douglas",country:"United States",cat:"historic",role:"Narrow-body airliner",status:"retired",first:1979,crew:2,pax:155,len:45.1,span:32.8,hgt:9.0,cruise:811,vmax:925,range:4635,ceil:11300,mtow:72575,engines:"2 × JT8D-200 turbofan (tail-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"tail",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The 'Mad Dog' — a stretched DC-9 with tail engines and a T-tail that defined American domestic flying through the 1980s and 90s. Loved by pilots for its honest handling; remembered by passengers for the quiet front and deafening back.",trivia:["American Airlines alone operated over 350 of them at the peak.","Seats ahead of the wing were whisper-quiet; the last rows sat beside the engines."],related:["boeing-727-200","boeing-737-800"]},
{slug:"dc-10-30",name:"McDonnell Douglas DC-10-30",maker:"McDonnell Douglas",country:"United States",cat:"historic",role:"Wide-body trijet",status:"retired",first:1970,crew:3,pax:270,len:55.5,span:50.4,hgt:17.7,cruise:908,vmax:982,range:9600,ceil:12800,mtow:263085,engines:"3 × CF6-50 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:3,m:"mix",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The trijet with the engine in its tail fin — built when ETOPS didn't exist and oceans demanded three engines. A rough safety start gave way to a long, redeemed career; its DNA lives on in the MD-11 freighters still flying cargo today.",trivia:["The number 3 engine sits inside the vertical fin — a configuration no one has repeated since.","The final passenger DC-10 flight was in 2014, with Biman Bangladesh."],related:["lockheed-l1011","boeing-747-8"]},
{slug:"lockheed-l1011",name:"Lockheed L-1011 TriStar",maker:"Lockheed",country:"United States",cat:"historic",role:"Wide-body trijet",status:"retired",first:1970,crew:3,pax:256,len:54.2,span:47.3,hgt:16.9,cruise:891,vmax:964,range:7419,ceil:13100,mtow:195045,engines:"3 × Rolls-Royce RB211 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:3,m:"mix",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The most technologically advanced airliner of its era — it could autoland in zero visibility in 1972. But its Rolls-Royce engine partner went bankrupt mid-program, the delays let the DC-10 take its market, and Lockheed never built an airliner again.",trivia:["Its autoland system was so good the FAA certified it for zero-visibility landings decades ahead of rivals.","One modified TriStar, 'Stargazer,' still launches Pegasus rockets from 39,000 ft."],related:["dc-10-30","boeing-747-8"]},
{slug:"de-havilland-comet",name:"de Havilland Comet 4",maker:"de Havilland",country:"United Kingdom",cat:"historic",role:"Jet airliner",status:"retired",first:1949,crew:4,pax:81,len:34.0,span:35.0,hgt:9.0,cruise:809,vmax:857,range:5190,ceil:12800,mtow:73480,engines:"4 × Avon turbojet (buried in wing roots)",p:{k:"jet",e:4,m:"buried",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The world's first jet airliner — beautiful, revolutionary, and tragic. Metal fatigue cracks from its square windows caused catastrophic failures in 1954; the exhaustive investigation that followed invented modern crash forensics and taught every aircraft since to have rounded windows.",trivia:["Its four engines were buried inside the wing roots — elegant, but a maintenance nightmare.","The Comet's fatigue investigation pioneered testing whole airframes in giant water tanks."],related:["boeing-707-320","sud-caravelle"]},
{slug:"sud-caravelle",name:"Sud Aviation Caravelle",maker:"Sud Aviation",country:"France",cat:"historic",role:"Jet airliner",status:"retired",first:1955,crew:3,pax:80,len:32.0,span:34.3,hgt:8.7,cruise:805,vmax:845,range:1845,ceil:12000,mtow:46000,engines:"2 × Avon turbojet (rear-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"tail",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The first jetliner with rear-mounted engines — a French invention that made the cabin dramatically quieter and was copied by the DC-9, 727, and every business jet since. It even borrowed the Comet's nose, licensed straight from de Havilland.",trivia:["Its triangular cabin windows remain unique in airliner history.","United Airlines flew Caravelles — a rare French jet in 1960s America."],related:["de-havilland-comet","md-80"]},
{slug:"tupolev-tu-154",name:"Tupolev Tu-154",maker:"Tupolev",country:"Soviet Union",cat:"historic",role:"Narrow-body trijet",status:"retired",first:1968,crew:4,pax:164,len:47.9,span:37.6,hgt:11.4,cruise:900,vmax:950,range:5280,ceil:12100,mtow:100000,engines:"3 × NK-8 / D-30 turbofan (tail-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:3,m:"tail",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The workhorse of the Soviet and post-Soviet skies — over a thousand built, fast, overbuilt, and designed to operate from rough Siberian runways that would destroy Western jets. For decades, flying in the USSR simply meant flying a Tu-154.",trivia:["Its landing gear retracts into distinctive pods on the wing trailing edge — a signature Tupolev trait.","Cruised faster than most Western contemporaries: around 900 km/h."],related:["boeing-727-200","md-80"]},
{slug:"antonov-an-124",name:"Antonov An-124 Ruslan",maker:"Antonov",country:"Ukraine (USSR)",cat:"cargo",role:"Strategic airlifter",status:"in_service",first:1982,crew:6,pax:0,len:69.1,span:73.3,hgt:21.1,cruise:800,vmax:865,range:4800,rangeNote:"with 120 t payload",ceil:12000,mtow:402000,engines:"4 × Progress D-18T turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:4,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"high"},story:"The An-225's smaller sibling and the largest production cargo aircraft in the world. Its nose swings up and its tail opens so vehicles can drive straight through; when something absurd needs to fly — locomotives, yachts, satellites — an An-124 gets the call.",trivia:["It kneels: the nose gear retracts partially so vehicles can drive up a ramp through the raised nose.","Both Ukraine and Russia operate fleets — on opposite sides since 2022."],related:["antonov-an-225","boeing-c-17"]},
{slug:"airbus-a400m",name:"Airbus A400M Atlas",maker:"Airbus",country:"European Union",cat:"cargo",role:"Military airlifter",status:"in_service",first:2009,crew:3,pax:0,len:45.1,span:42.4,hgt:14.7,cruise:780,vmax:825,range:3298,rangeNote:"at max payload",ceil:11300,mtow:141000,engines:"4 × TP400-D6 turboprop",p:{k:"prop",e:4,m:"wing",t:"t",w:"high"},story:"Europe's answer to the C-130 and C-17 in one machine — turboprop efficiency with near-jet speed. Its eight-bladed props on each side rotate toward each other in pairs, an aerodynamic trick that steadies the airflow over the wing.",trivia:["Its TP400 engines are the most powerful turboprops in the Western world.","Adjacent propellers counter-rotate — look closely and the blades sweep toward each other."],related:["lockheed-c-130","boeing-c-17"]},
{slug:"boeing-c-17",name:"Boeing C-17 Globemaster III",maker:"Boeing (McDonnell Douglas)",country:"United States",cat:"cargo",role:"Strategic airlifter",status:"in_service",first:1991,crew:3,pax:0,len:53.0,span:51.8,hgt:16.8,cruise:830,vmax:917,range:4480,rangeNote:"with 71 t payload",ceil:13700,mtow:265352,engines:"4 × F117 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:4,m:"wing",t:"t",w:"high"},story:"The aircraft that shows up when history happens — tanks to war zones, aid to disasters, evacuations from Kabul. It lands a 77-tonne payload on a 1,000 m dirt strip and can even back up under its own power using reverse thrust.",trivia:["One C-17 carried 823 people out of Kabul in a single 2021 flight — a record born of desperation.","It can reverse uphill on a 2% slope using thrust reversers."],related:["antonov-an-124","lockheed-c-130"]},
{slug:"f-16-fighting-falcon",name:"General Dynamics F-16 Fighting Falcon",maker:"General Dynamics / Lockheed Martin",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"Multirole fighter",status:"in_service",first:1974,crew:1,pax:0,len:15.0,span:9.4,hgt:5.1,cruise:930,vmax:2120,range:550,rangeNote:"combat radius · ~4,200 km ferry",ceil:15240,mtow:19187,engines:"1 × F110 / F100 turbofan",p:{k:"fighter",e:1,delta:0},story:"The most numerous fighter in the Western world — over 4,600 built, flown by 25+ countries, and still in production five decades on. It was the first fighter deliberately built unstable and kept flyable only by computers, trading stability for agility.",trivia:["Its reclined seat and side-stick help pilots endure 9g turns.","Deliberately aerodynamically unstable — without its flight computers it cannot be flown."],related:["f-35-lightning-ii","f-22-raptor"]},
{slug:"f-35-lightning-ii",name:"Lockheed Martin F-35A Lightning II",maker:"Lockheed Martin",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"Stealth multirole fighter",status:"in_service",first:2006,crew:1,pax:0,len:15.7,span:10.7,hgt:4.4,cruise:915,vmax:1930,range:1093,rangeNote:"combat radius",ceil:15240,mtow:31800,engines:"1 × F135 turbofan",p:{k:"fighter",e:1},story:"The largest defense program in history — a stealth fighter built in three versions (runway, jump-jet, carrier) for a dozen nations. Less a plane than a flying sensor network: its helmet lets pilots literally see through the airframe via external cameras.",trivia:["The F135 is the most powerful fighter engine ever built (~190 kN with afterburner).","Its $400,000 helmet displays camera feeds so the pilot can look 'through' the floor."],related:["f-22-raptor","f-16-fighting-falcon"]},
{slug:"eurofighter-typhoon",name:"Eurofighter Typhoon",maker:"Eurofighter consortium",country:"UK / Germany / Italy / Spain",cat:"military",role:"Multirole fighter",status:"in_service",first:1994,crew:1,pax:0,len:16.0,span:11.0,hgt:5.3,cruise:1100,cruiseNote:"supercruise capable",vmax:2495,range:1389,rangeNote:"combat radius (air defence)",ceil:16765,mtow:23500,engines:"2 × EJ200 turbofan",p:{k:"fighter",e:2,delta:1,can:1},story:"Europe's four-nation delta-canard fighter — deliberately unstable, blisteringly quick to accelerate and climb, and the backbone of air policing across the continent. Its canards (small front wings) give it its unmistakable silhouette.",trivia:["Built by a four-country consortium; each nation assembles its own aircraft.","Can supercruise above Mach 1 without afterburner."],related:["dassault-rafale","f-22-raptor"]},
{slug:"dassault-rafale",name:"Dassault Rafale",maker:"Dassault Aviation",country:"France",cat:"military",role:"Multirole fighter",status:"in_service",first:1986,crew:1,pax:0,len:15.3,span:10.8,hgt:5.3,cruise:1000,vmax:1912,range:1850,rangeNote:"penetration mission",ceil:15240,mtow:24500,engines:"2 × Snecma M88 turbofan",p:{k:"fighter",e:2,delta:1,can:1},story:"France's do-everything fighter — one airframe for air combat, strike, reconnaissance, carrier operations, and nuclear deterrence. Its export career caught fire in the 2020s with India, Egypt, the UAE, and half a dozen others signing on.",trivia:["Flies France's airborne nuclear deterrent mission.","The naval version lands on France's carrier Charles de Gaulle with no major structural differences."],related:["eurofighter-typhoon","mig-29"]},
{slug:"mig-29",name:"Mikoyan MiG-29",maker:"Mikoyan",country:"Soviet Union",cat:"military",role:"Air-superiority fighter",status:"in_service",first:1977,crew:1,pax:0,len:17.3,span:11.4,hgt:4.7,cruise:850,vmax:2400,range:1430,rangeNote:"internal fuel",ceil:18000,mtow:18000,engines:"2 × RD-33 turbofan",p:{k:"fighter",e:2},story:"The Soviet counterpunch to the F-16 — brutally agile up close, with a helmet-cued missile system the West didn't match for 15 years. Louvered auxiliary intakes let it operate from rough, debris-strewn runways with its main intakes closed.",trivia:["On rough strips it breathes through louvers atop the wing roots, main intakes sealed against debris.","After German reunification, NATO pilots flying ex-East German MiG-29s were stunned by its close-range agility."],related:["f-16-fighting-falcon","dassault-rafale"]},
{slug:"p-51-mustang",name:"North American P-51D Mustang",maker:"North American Aviation",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"Fighter",status:"retired",first:1940,crew:1,pax:0,len:9.8,span:11.3,hgt:4.1,cruise:580,vmax:703,range:2755,rangeNote:"with drop tanks",ceil:12770,mtow:5490,engines:"1 × Packard V-1650 Merlin piston",p:{k:"prop",e:1,m:"nose",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The fighter that changed the air war over Europe — with drop tanks it could escort bombers all the way to Berlin and back, something no Allied fighter could do before it. An American airframe married to the British Merlin engine made it legendary.",trivia:["Designed and prototyped in just 102 days.","Its laminar-flow wing and clever radiator design actually produced slight thrust — the 'Meredith effect'."],related:["supermarine-spitfire","messerschmitt-bf-109"]},
{slug:"messerschmitt-bf-109",name:"Messerschmitt Bf 109",maker:"Messerschmitt",country:"Germany",cat:"military",role:"Fighter",status:"retired",first:1935,crew:1,pax:0,len:9.0,span:9.9,hgt:2.6,cruise:590,vmax:640,range:850,ceil:12000,mtow:3400,engines:"1 × Daimler-Benz DB 605 piston",p:{k:"prop",e:1,m:"nose",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The most-produced fighter aircraft in history — roughly 34,000 built. The Spitfire's great rival was small, fast, and constantly evolved through a decade of war; its fuel-injected engine could pitch into dives that starved carbureted opponents' engines.",trivia:["About 34,000 built — no fighter has ever matched the number.","Its narrow landing gear caused thousands of ground accidents — a known flaw never fully fixed."],related:["supermarine-spitfire","p-51-mustang"]},
{slug:"b-17-flying-fortress",name:"Boeing B-17 Flying Fortress",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"Heavy bomber",status:"retired",first:1935,crew:10,pax:0,len:22.7,span:31.6,hgt:5.8,cruise:293,vmax:462,range:3219,ceil:10850,mtow:29710,engines:"4 × Wright R-1820 radial piston",p:{k:"prop",e:4,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The bomber that carried the American daylight air campaign over Europe — bristling with 13 machine guns and famous for bringing crews home with impossible damage: missing tails, shredded wings, two engines gone. Roughly 12,700 were built.",trivia:["B-17s returned home with damage so severe engineers used them to study structural redundancy.","A journalist coined 'Flying Fortress' on seeing the prototype's gun positions — Boeing trademarked it."],related:["douglas-dc-3","b-52-stratofortress"]},
{slug:"mitsubishi-a6m-zero",name:"Mitsubishi A6M Zero",maker:"Mitsubishi",country:"Japan",cat:"military",role:"Carrier fighter",status:"retired",first:1939,crew:1,pax:0,len:9.1,span:12.0,hgt:3.1,cruise:333,vmax:533,range:3105,ceil:10000,mtow:2796,engines:"1 × Nakajima Sakae radial piston",p:{k:"prop",e:1,m:"nose",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"In 1940 the Zero could out-turn and out-range every fighter on Earth — achieved by ruthlessly deleting weight, including armor and self-sealing fuel tanks. That bargain made it dominant early in the Pacific war and tragically fragile once Allied tactics adapted.",trivia:["Its 3,100 km range stunned Allied planners — fighters weren't supposed to reach that far.","Weight-saving went as far as drilling holes in structural brackets."],related:["supermarine-spitfire","p-51-mustang"]},
{slug:"b-52-stratofortress",name:"Boeing B-52H Stratofortress",maker:"Boeing",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"Strategic bomber",status:"in_service",first:1952,crew:5,pax:0,len:48.5,span:56.4,hgt:12.4,cruise:819,vmax:1047,range:14080,ceil:15000,mtow:220000,engines:"8 × TF33 turbofan (re-engining to F130)",p:{k:"jet",e:8,m:"wing",t:"conv",w:"high"},story:"The bomber that refuses to retire — first flown in 1952 and now scheduled to serve past 2050, meaning a century of service on one airframe design. New Rolls-Royce engines and radars are going into aircraft older than the grandparents of their pilots.",trivia:["Some B-52 pilots fly the exact tail numbers their grandfathers flew.","Eight engines in four twin pods — the last eight-engine aircraft in military service."],related:["b-2-spirit","boeing-c-17"]},
{slug:"b-2-spirit",name:"Northrop Grumman B-2 Spirit",maker:"Northrop Grumman",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"Stealth bomber",status:"in_service",first:1989,crew:2,pax:0,len:21.0,span:52.4,hgt:5.2,cruise:900,vmax:1010,range:11000,ceil:15200,mtow:170600,engines:"4 × F118 turbofan (buried)",p:{k:"wing"},story:"A flying wing with no tail, no fuselage, and a radar signature reportedly the size of a bird — each one costs over $2 billion. Only 21 were built; they fly 30+ hour global missions from Missouri with two pilots and a cot.",trivia:["Crews pack cots and food for missions exceeding 30 hours nonstop.","Its shape has no vertical surfaces at all — computers command split ailerons to steer."],related:["lockheed-sr-71","b-52-stratofortress"]},
{slug:"lockheed-u-2",name:"Lockheed U-2 Dragon Lady",maker:"Lockheed Skunk Works",country:"United States",cat:"military",role:"High-altitude reconnaissance",status:"in_service",first:1955,crew:1,pax:0,len:19.2,span:31.4,hgt:4.9,cruise:760,vmax:805,range:11280,ceil:21300,mtow:18600,engines:"1 × F118 turbofan",p:{k:"jet",e:1,m:"buried",t:"conv",w:"mid"},story:"Essentially a jet-powered glider that cruises at 21 km, where pilots wear full spacesuits. At altitude it flies in the 'coffin corner' — stall speed and maximum speed just a few knots apart — and landing it requires a chase car calling out the final feet.",trivia:["A high-performance chase car races down the runway on every landing, radioing height to the pilot.","At cruise altitude, the margin between stalling and overspeed can be under 10 knots."],related:["lockheed-sr-71","b-2-spirit"]},
{slug:"gulfstream-g650",name:"Gulfstream G650ER",maker:"Gulfstream",country:"United States",cat:"general",role:"Business jet",status:"in_service",first:2009,crew:2,pax:19,len:30.4,span:30.4,hgt:7.8,cruise:904,vmax:982,range:13890,ceil:15545,mtow:46992,engines:"2 × BR725 turbofan (rear-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"tail",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The flagship of business aviation — Mach 0.925 capability, 13,900 km of range, and a cabin pressurized lower than any airliner. When it launched, used G650s sold above list price; there was a years-long waiting list to skip the waiting list.",trivia:["Set a westbound around-the-world record: 41 hours, 7 minutes.","Its oval cabin windows are the largest in business aviation."],related:["cessna-citation-x","boeing-787-9"]},
{slug:"cessna-citation-x",name:"Cessna Citation X+",maker:"Cessna",country:"United States",cat:"general",role:"Business jet",status:"in_service",first:1993,crew:2,pax:12,len:22.4,span:21.1,hgt:5.9,cruise:972,vmax:993,range:6408,ceil:15545,mtow:16601,engines:"2 × AE3007C2 turbofan (rear-mounted)",p:{k:"jet",e:2,m:"tail",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The fastest civilian aircraft in service since Concorde retired — Mach 0.935, faster than any airliner. Golfer Arnold Palmer took delivery of the first one; its highly swept wing looks borrowed from a fighter.",trivia:["Max operating speed Mach 0.935 — it routinely gets direct routings just to stay out of airliners' way.","Its wing sweep (37°) is greater than a 787's."],related:["gulfstream-g650","concorde"]},
{slug:"beechcraft-king-air-350",name:"Beechcraft King Air 350",maker:"Beechcraft",country:"United States",cat:"general",role:"Utility turboprop",status:"in_service",first:1988,crew:2,pax:9,len:14.2,span:17.7,hgt:4.4,cruise:578,vmax:640,range:3345,ceil:10668,mtow:6804,engines:"2 × PT6A-60A turboprop",p:{k:"prop",e:2,m:"wing",t:"t",w:"low"},story:"The default twin turboprop of the world — air ambulance, corporate shuttle, military trainer, survey platform. The King Air family has been in continuous production since 1964, longer than any other civilian aircraft line.",trivia:["The longest continuous production run in civil aviation — over 60 years of King Airs.","Its PT6 engine family is so reliable it's often called 'the engine that never quits'."],related:["cessna-172","atr-72-600"]},
{slug:"piper-j3-cub",name:"Piper J-3 Cub",maker:"Piper Aircraft",country:"United States",cat:"historic",role:"Light trainer",status:"retired",first:1938,crew:1,pax:1,len:6.8,span:10.7,hgt:2.0,cruise:121,vmax:140,range:354,ceil:3600,mtow:550,engines:"1 × Continental A-65 piston",p:{k:"prop",e:1,m:"nose",t:"conv",w:"high"},story:"The airplane that taught America to fly — 20,000 built, and 75% of all US WWII pilots touched one in training. Bright 'Cub Yellow,' 65 horsepower, and so slow and honest that instructors joked it could 'just barely kill you.'",trivia:["Flown solo from the rear seat — the balance requires it.","Its trademark yellow paint is an official color: 'Cub Yellow' (Lock Haven Yellow)."],related:["cessna-172","douglas-dc-3"]},
{slug:"dhc-2-beaver",name:"De Havilland Canada DHC-2 Beaver",maker:"De Havilland Canada",country:"Canada",cat:"general",role:"Bush plane",status:"in_service",first:1947,crew:1,pax:7,len:9.2,span:14.6,hgt:2.7,cruise:230,vmax:255,range:732,ceil:5486,mtow:2313,engines:"1 × P&W R-985 radial piston",p:{k:"prop",e:1,m:"nose",t:"conv",w:"high"},story:"The definitive bush plane — designed by asking Canadian wilderness pilots what they needed, then building exactly that. On wheels, skis, or floats it opened the North; 75-year-old Beavers still fly scheduled service in Alaska and British Columbia daily.",trivia:["Engineers added doors wide enough for a fuel drum because bush pilots asked.","Harrison Ford calls his Beaver his favorite aircraft."],related:["cessna-172","piper-j3-cub"]},
{slug:"cirrus-sr22",name:"Cirrus SR22",maker:"Cirrus Aircraft",country:"United States",cat:"general",role:"Light aircraft",status:"in_service",first:2001,crew:1,pax:4,len:7.9,span:11.7,hgt:2.7,cruise:339,vmax:378,range:1943,ceil:5334,mtow:1633,engines:"1 × Continental IO-550 piston",p:{k:"prop",e:1,m:"nose",t:"conv",w:"low"},story:"The best-selling single-engine piston aircraft of the 21st century — and the one with a parachute for the entire airframe. Pull the red handle and the whole aircraft descends under canopy; the system has saved over 250 lives to date.",trivia:["Its CAPS whole-airframe parachute has recorded 130+ successful deployments.","Outsells every other piston single, every year, for two decades running."],related:["cessna-172","beechcraft-king-air-350"]}
